<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nubians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nubians are a people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> who speak Nubian languages. Their ancestors created the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerma">Kerma</a> and Kush and the Christian states of Nobadia, Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>, before gradual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabisation">Arabisation</a> from the 14th century. In the 1960s the reservoir of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> flooded much of their homeland, displacing tens of thousands of people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nubians.jpg" alt="Nubians" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mohammed Moussa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nubians_at_Aswan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nubians descend from the early inhabitants of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements in the central Nubian region date back to 7000 BCE, and by about 6000 BCE peoples in the region had developed an agricultural economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> was at times part of Pharaonic Egypt and at times a rival state, and by the Twenty-fifth Dynasty (744 to 656 BCE) all of Egypt was united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. The ancient Nubians were known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> of great skill and precision.</p><p>The modern name is debated. An older view derives it from the Egyptian word for gold, but this has no support in Egyptian texts, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>'s Institute for the Study of Ancient Cultures traces it instead to Greek forms of the name of the Noba or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobades">Nobatae</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kingdom of Kush</a> remained a major power until the fourth century CE, when it weakened amid worsening climate, internal rebellion and invasion, notably by the Noba, who introduced the Nubian languages and gave their name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>. Aksumite forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>, and the Kushite realm broke into the three kingdoms of Nobadia, Makuria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alodia">Alodia</a>. Their people gradually became Christian, and frescoes made between 800 and 1200 CE in cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> followed the Byzantine style.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> from the eighth century. In 651 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> invaded Makuria and was repulsed twice, and a treaty known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt</a> barred further invasions in return for 360 slaves a year and required the Nubians to maintain a mosque for Muslim visitors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> preachers settled in Nubia from the late fourteenth century, and by the sixteenth century most Nubians had converted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Nubians speak Nubian languages of the Nilo-Saharan family as their mother tongue, and Arabic as a second language. The main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> groups, from north to south, are the Kenzi, Faddicca, Halfawi, Sukkot, Mahas and Danagla. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> they live mainly in Aswan Governorate, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in the Northern State. Today Nubians practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syncretism">syncretism</a> of Islam and traditional folk belief.</p><p>The building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> in the 1960s flooded much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a> and forced many Nubians to move, some to villages in New Nubia near Kom Ombo and others from Wadi Halfa to Khashm el Girba and New Halfa. Nubians share an identity expressed in poetry, novels, music and storytelling.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nubians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An Aksumite inscription records an expedition against the warlike <b>Nubians</b> that marched to the confluence of the Nile and the Atbara and plundered Kushite towns, among them one named Alwa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 642 a raiding force sent by Amr ibn al-As, probably led by Uqba ibn Nafi, met fierce resistance, and Arab writers remembered the <b>Nubians</b> as rumat al-hadaq, the pupil-smiters, because their archers aimed at the eyes of their enemies and blinded many.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Senusret I campaigned as far as the Second Cataract, and Senusret III led several expeditions south, fixing the frontier at Semna with boundary stelae that forbade <b>Nubians</b> to pass northwards except to trade, and clearing a canal through the First Cataract for his boats.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Banu Kanz descended from Arabs of the Rabi'a tribe who settled in the Aswan region and the gold mines of the Eastern Desert in the 9th century and intermarried with <b>Nubians</b> and Beja.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-kanz-emirate">Banu Kanz emirate</a></li></ul></section>
